WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Welcome everyone. In today's lecture we'll be looking at probabilistic motion models.

00:05.000 --> 00:13.000
What this essentially deals with is to understand the uncertainty that's involved while a robot is moving.

00:13.000 --> 00:17.000
So the uncertainty in the motion can come due to multiple reasons.

00:17.000 --> 00:23.000
This could be because, let's say, the wheels of the robot slip on the floor,

00:23.000 --> 00:28.000
or this could be due to the fact that we give a certain command to the robot,

00:28.000 --> 00:32.000
but the robot doesn't reach this velocity instantaneously.

00:32.000 --> 00:37.000
So the motion that the robot executes is not the one that we actually expect it to do.

00:37.000 --> 00:43.000
And this is one of the many reasons that introduces uncertainty in the motion for a robot.

00:43.000 --> 00:51.000
In the previous lecture, if you remember, what we saw was basically how different types of robots,

00:51.000 --> 00:58.000
so mainly different wheeled robots, move based on the control input that we give to them.

00:58.000 --> 01:02.000
So we saw how differential drive robots work, we saw how Ackermann robots work,

01:02.000 --> 01:07.000
but there we always assume that the control will be followed by the robot perfectly.

01:07.000 --> 01:12.000
In today's lecture what's changing is that this motion is a bit uncertain,

01:12.000 --> 01:15.000
and we want to understand how to model this uncertainty.

01:15.000 --> 01:22.000
And generally we'll be using simple probabilistic models to capture this uncertainty in the motion.

01:22.000 --> 01:28.000
And what we'll also look at is how these motion models, the so-called motion models,

01:28.000 --> 01:31.000
can then be used for state estimation problems.

01:31.000 --> 01:36.000
Since now we can both model the movement and the uncertainty in the movement,

01:36.000 --> 01:41.000
we'll see how this fits into our Bayesian framework for state estimation.

01:41.000 --> 01:43.000
Okay, so let's get started.

01:43.000 --> 01:50.000
The main theme in this lecture is basically the fact that the motion is inherently uncertain,

01:50.000 --> 01:53.000
and this uncertainty comes from multiple reasons.

01:53.000 --> 02:00.000
So for example, if I ask the robot to move one meter forward, in practice maybe it just moves 95 cm,

02:00.000 --> 02:04.000
or 1.1 m and 5 cm and so on.

02:04.000 --> 02:09.000
So this could happen, for example, typically for example in a differential robot,

02:09.000 --> 02:16.000
we set the velocity for the left and the right wheels, and this drives the robot.

02:16.000 --> 02:21.000
However, suppose we ask the motors to rotate at a particular speed,

02:21.000 --> 02:24.000
it may not actually achieve this speed instantaneously.

02:24.000 --> 02:31.000
This results in the fact that the distance that it travels is less or more than we actually expect it to be.

02:31.000 --> 02:37.000
And the question that we will look at today is how to then model this uncertainty out there.

02:37.000 --> 02:41.000
So just to have an example here, what we see here is a maze environment,

02:41.000 --> 02:46.000
and the robot starts here and then goes around this maze, all around basically.

02:46.000 --> 02:53.000
And the red line here actually shows the path that's actually traversed by that robot.

02:53.000 --> 02:59.000
However, the robot thinks that it actually traverses this other red line that you see here.

02:59.000 --> 03:06.000
And what's happening here is that the internal estimate, or the odometry of the robot, is drifting.

03:06.000 --> 03:13.000
So there seems to be a tendency to always estimate that it goes a bit right, then it's actually going.

03:13.000 --> 03:19.000
This may be because of an imbalance in the way the weight is distributed on the robot.

03:19.000 --> 03:24.000
Maybe one of the wheels has more tire pressure than the other one.

03:24.000 --> 03:28.000
What we end up with is kind of a systematic drift over time,

03:28.000 --> 03:33.000
and this is, so to say, the uncertainty that's involved while executing motion.

03:33.000 --> 03:38.000
So the question that we ask here is given the actual movement here,

03:38.000 --> 03:46.000
and the odometry or the estimate of the model here, how are these two linked?

03:46.000 --> 03:50.000
So how can we actually map this uncertainty between them?

03:50.000 --> 03:55.000
And if you think of it in terms of probability, what we are exactly calculating

03:55.000 --> 04:00.000
is the probability of a robot ending up at Xt given,

04:00.000 --> 04:09.000
so this line here is read as given, given that you start at Xt-1 and execute a particular command Ut.

04:09.000 --> 04:13.000
And this probability distribution is also called as motion model,

04:13.000 --> 04:21.000
because essentially it models if you start at Xt-1, what's the probability that you end up at Xt?

04:21.000 --> 04:26.000
And if you again think of this example where I asked the robot to move one meter,

04:27.000 --> 04:35.000
this probability would, for example, be meaningful to ask, say, around one meter, say, at 1.1 meter,

04:35.000 --> 04:43.000
what's the probability that I end up at, say, Xt equals to 1.1, given, for example, we start at the origin?

04:43.000 --> 04:50.000
And so this probability we can compute for different positions in the world.

04:50.000 --> 04:56.000
So we can compute it at 1, we can compute it at 1.1, we can compute it at 0.99, and so on.

04:56.000 --> 05:00.000
So what we expect is that if we ask the robot to move one meter,

05:00.000 --> 05:04.000
the highest probability should be around one meter mark.

05:04.000 --> 05:10.000
And what we basically encode using the whole probability is what's the distribution or where,

05:10.000 --> 05:17.000
what's the probability at different points or different locations in the environment for the robot to land up in.

05:18.000 --> 05:23.000
And the way this relates to our state estimation framework is in the following way.

05:23.000 --> 05:27.000
So if you recall from the lecture from recursive Bayes filter,

05:27.000 --> 05:31.000
we had come up with a recursive formulation for estimating the belief.

05:31.000 --> 05:35.000
So this is the probability that the robot is at Xt.

05:35.000 --> 05:39.000
And we had come up with a formulation that depends or is recursive,

05:39.000 --> 05:45.000
so that depends on the belief at Xt-1 and is then modified by a certain parameter here.

05:45.000 --> 05:47.000
So there are two parameters here.

05:47.000 --> 05:56.000
One, the one which is the focus of this lecture is to understand how you move basically from Xt-1 to Xt.

05:56.000 --> 06:02.000
So essentially what this equation is saying is that go over all your previous positions,

06:02.000 --> 06:10.000
so Xt-1s, then multiply them with your motion model probabilities of ending up in a particular place,

06:10.000 --> 06:16.000
sum them up, and then we will see what this particular term means later on.

06:16.000 --> 06:22.000
So this is the place where we need this knowledge of how the robot moves

06:22.000 --> 06:27.000
or how the probability distribution is when a particular action is made.

06:27.000 --> 06:31.000
And if you remember again that we had these two main steps,

06:31.000 --> 06:34.000
a prediction step and a correction step in the Bayes filter estimate.

06:34.000 --> 06:38.000
And what we just saw is what is also called as the prediction step

06:38.000 --> 06:43.000
because we are looking at all the positions, so all the positions Xt-1,

06:43.000 --> 06:49.000
and we're looking at the probability at Xt-1 and then propagating it through the model,

06:49.000 --> 06:56.000
so through the way we propagate this motion, the control that's given by ut

06:56.000 --> 07:02.000
to give the predicted belief here denoted by the bar on top.

07:02.000 --> 07:07.000
And then what would be the main focus of the lecture on observation model

07:07.000 --> 07:13.000
would then be how does the measurements taken by your sensor then modify this belief.

07:13.000 --> 07:16.000
So typically what happens is that you make a movement.

07:16.000 --> 07:19.000
This movement usually introduces some uncertainty.

07:19.000 --> 07:22.000
And then you take a particular measurement Zt.

07:22.000 --> 07:25.000
This could be, for example, a GPS measurement.

07:25.000 --> 07:27.000
This could be from a laser scanner.

07:27.000 --> 07:29.000
This could be from different sources.

07:29.000 --> 07:36.000
And often it works in the way that the motion model or the motion step introduces more uncertainty,

07:36.000 --> 07:38.000
whereas the measurement step decreases this.

07:38.000 --> 07:44.000
So this is the way in which these two models help us estimate the state of, for example,

07:44.000 --> 07:47.000
where the robot is in the environment.

07:47.000 --> 07:52.000
So to be a bit more formal, when we talk about motion model and, in particular here,

07:52.000 --> 07:57.000
the probabilistic motion model, what we want to compute is the posterior probability.

07:57.000 --> 08:03.000
It's posterior because we want to find out what's the probability of Xt given certain conditions.

08:03.000 --> 08:10.000
And this condition is that given that you're at Xt-1 and you apply a certain command Ut,

08:10.000 --> 08:13.000
what's the probability that you will end up at Xt?

08:13.000 --> 08:17.000
So if you think of the different drives that we saw in the previous lecture

08:17.000 --> 08:22.000
or in the lecture on locomotion, this Ut could be, in a differential drive case,

08:22.000 --> 08:26.000
the velocities given to the left wheel and the right wheel.

08:26.000 --> 08:31.000
If you think of an Ackerman drive, this could be the linear velocity, the angular velocity,

08:31.000 --> 08:35.000
as well as the steering that you set for your robot.

08:35.000 --> 08:40.000
And it holds not just for wheeled robots, but also for robots in general.

08:40.000 --> 08:45.000
This could be, for example, a quadrocopter where you give different commands to the propellers

08:45.000 --> 08:48.000
that you have, a humanoid or whatever.

08:48.000 --> 08:53.000
The main idea is when you give a particular command, what's the probability distribution

08:53.000 --> 08:58.000
of ending up at different places in the environment?

08:58.000 --> 09:02.000
So what we'll focus in today's lecture is mainly on wheeled robots,

09:02.000 --> 09:12.000
and we will see two commonly used models which kind of abstract different motions for wheeled platforms.

09:12.000 --> 09:17.000
And these two typical models are what are called as the odometry-based models

09:17.000 --> 09:23.000
and velocity-based model, also sometimes called as the dead reckoning approach.

09:23.000 --> 09:25.000
The difference being the following.

09:25.000 --> 09:30.000
In the odometry-based model, we set a particular command to our robot.

09:30.000 --> 09:32.000
Then it executes this motion.

09:32.000 --> 09:37.000
What we do then is to attach some kind of a sensor to the motor shaft

09:37.000 --> 09:40.000
to understand how this motor is rotating.

09:40.000 --> 09:46.000
Then we use this information to figure out how much a platform has moved.

09:46.000 --> 09:50.000
So in a way, it's a feedback system where we put the control

09:50.000 --> 09:55.000
to see actually how the platform has moved.

09:55.000 --> 09:59.000
So technically speaking, you could treat this as a measurement,

09:59.000 --> 10:05.000
but the way we will treat this in this course is to treat odometry as basically a control command

10:05.000 --> 10:09.000
because it's a measurement of how basically we moved.

10:09.000 --> 10:15.000
And the second model which is also quite commonly used is what is called as the velocity-based model.

10:15.000 --> 10:18.000
In a sense, this is just a look-ahead step

10:18.000 --> 10:20.000
in the sense you don't actually measure anything.

10:20.000 --> 10:23.000
You say to the robot, okay, go with this velocity

10:23.000 --> 10:26.000
and say linear velocity of one meter per second

10:26.000 --> 10:29.000
and an angular velocity of three degrees per second.

10:29.000 --> 10:33.000
And then you just use your physics of your platform

10:33.000 --> 10:36.000
to figure out where you end up with.

10:36.000 --> 10:38.000
We can see this a bit more in detail.

10:38.000 --> 10:42.000
This is related to the odometry model.

10:42.000 --> 10:47.000
And what you see here is kind of the setup that you have for wheel encoders.

10:47.000 --> 10:51.000
So what happens is you usually use this kind of disc

10:51.000 --> 10:53.000
which has alternating white and black lines,

10:53.000 --> 10:56.000
and you attach this to the shaft of a motor.

10:56.000 --> 11:01.000
And therefore when the motor rotates, this particular disc also rotates.

11:01.000 --> 11:04.000
And then there is some associated electronics

11:04.000 --> 11:10.000
which figure out when you move from a white line to a black line.

11:10.000 --> 11:12.000
Often it results in a peak.

11:12.000 --> 11:14.000
And therefore what we can do is to figure out

11:14.000 --> 11:17.000
exactly how much the motor has rotated.

11:17.000 --> 11:20.000
And then understanding how much the motor has rotated,

11:20.000 --> 11:24.000
we can then translate this into how much the platform itself has moved

11:24.000 --> 11:29.000
as, let's say for example, the wheels are directly attached to the motors and so on.

11:29.000 --> 11:32.000
So when we talk about odometry models,

11:32.000 --> 11:36.000
usually the robots are equipped with some kind of encoders

11:36.000 --> 11:41.000
which give us, so to say, an internal state of how the motors are moving.

11:41.000 --> 11:45.000
On the other hand, when we talk about the velocity-based model,

11:45.000 --> 11:48.000
or what is called as dead reckoning,

11:48.000 --> 11:51.000
what we do there is in a way a prediction.

11:51.000 --> 11:56.000
So we know how our platform moves when a certain control command is given.

11:56.000 --> 12:00.000
And we just use the equations that we developed,

12:00.000 --> 12:03.000
for example, for the differential drive of an Ackerman drive.

12:03.000 --> 12:06.000
And then we figure out where the robot would end.

12:06.000 --> 12:11.000
In practice, the odometry model often gives

12:11.000 --> 12:16.000
a better or higher accuracy measurement than the dead reckoning,

12:16.000 --> 12:20.000
because in a way the odometry model captures the rotations

12:20.000 --> 12:23.000
after the command has been executed,

12:23.000 --> 12:27.000
where in the dead reckoning model you just wish that,

12:27.000 --> 12:29.000
or you hope that this particular command is executed,

12:29.000 --> 12:32.000
but you actually don't know what happened in between.

12:32.000 --> 12:38.000
So in practice, we often use the odometry model to do the motion.

12:38.000 --> 12:42.000
And now what we look at is why or from where

12:42.000 --> 12:46.000
does this uncertainty in the motion come from.

12:46.000 --> 12:49.000
And in particular, we will see this for wheel robots.

12:49.000 --> 12:52.000
So the ideal case is, for example, here,

12:52.000 --> 12:56.000
where we have the two wheels of exactly the same diameter,

12:56.000 --> 13:00.000
and it's flat, and if it rolls smoothly on the floor,

13:00.000 --> 13:04.000
you would expect that it makes the movement that is, say,

13:04.000 --> 13:06.000
defined by the differential drive robot,

13:06.000 --> 13:08.000
by the differential drive equations,

13:08.000 --> 13:10.000
or by the Ackerman equations.

13:10.000 --> 13:12.000
But in practice, what can happen is that

13:12.000 --> 13:14.000
as it moves in the environment,

13:14.000 --> 13:18.000
say it goes over a piece of wire or a stone or whatever,

13:18.000 --> 13:21.000
and then this causes there to be a bump.

13:21.000 --> 13:24.000
And what often happens is that you,

13:24.000 --> 13:27.000
for example, in this case, this right wheel here

13:27.000 --> 13:29.000
would skip certain revolutions.

13:29.000 --> 13:32.000
And with this, you would end up thinking that

13:32.000 --> 13:35.000
you covered a different amount of distance

13:35.000 --> 13:38.000
than you actually did.

13:38.000 --> 13:40.000
What can also happen is, for example,

13:40.000 --> 13:42.000
you may have different wheel diameters.

13:42.000 --> 13:45.000
This could be because of, let's say,

13:45.000 --> 13:48.000
we pump the two wheels differently.

13:48.000 --> 13:50.000
This would be a stupid thing to do,

13:50.000 --> 13:52.000
but this is one way this could happen.

13:52.000 --> 13:54.000
The other way would be, for example,

13:54.000 --> 13:57.000
usually the robots would carry some kind of a load,

13:57.000 --> 14:00.000
and then if the load is not evenly distributed,

14:00.000 --> 14:02.000
it might happen that there's more pressure

14:02.000 --> 14:04.000
on one side than the other.

14:04.000 --> 14:07.000
Whereas when we use equations of motion,

14:07.000 --> 14:10.000
we might not know this,

14:10.000 --> 14:13.000
and what we end up in terms of an estimate of where we go

14:13.000 --> 14:17.000
is different from the actual motion that the robot performs.

14:17.000 --> 14:22.000
Also, it could happen that we are moving on platforms,

14:22.000 --> 14:25.000
maybe in this case a carpet or a slippery floor,

14:25.000 --> 14:30.000
and the wheels may not be perfectly rolling on this surface.

14:30.000 --> 14:32.000
And in this case, again, what happens is that

14:32.000 --> 14:34.000
we are not able to perfectly figure out

14:34.000 --> 14:36.000
how much the wheel has rotated,

14:36.000 --> 14:38.000
and therefore we make a mistake

14:38.000 --> 14:42.000
in seeing how far the robot has actually moved.

14:42.000 --> 14:44.000
So what we'll do in this lecture is that

14:44.000 --> 14:48.000
we'll abstract a bit away from the exact drive,

14:48.000 --> 14:50.000
so we will not talk specifically about

14:50.000 --> 14:52.000
differential robot RKM,

14:52.000 --> 14:54.000
but we'll see two models which kind of

14:54.000 --> 14:56.000
sit on top of this,

14:56.000 --> 14:58.000
which kind of generalize this,

14:58.000 --> 15:01.000
and it also makes our analysis for the,

15:01.000 --> 15:04.000
for deriving the equations a bit easier.

15:04.000 --> 15:07.000
And the first of this model, like we said before,

15:07.000 --> 15:10.000
is what is called as the odometry model.

15:10.000 --> 15:12.000
So in this model, what happens is,

15:12.000 --> 15:15.000
suppose at time t-1, we start at this position,

15:15.000 --> 15:17.000
x-bar, y-bar, and theta-bar,

15:17.000 --> 15:21.000
and at time t-1, we end up at the

15:21.000 --> 15:25.000
x-prime, y-prime, and theta-prime positions.

15:25.000 --> 15:29.000
And the way we would now express the

15:29.000 --> 15:32.000
odometry command, so to say,

15:32.000 --> 15:34.000
is by these three motions.

15:34.000 --> 15:37.000
We say that the motion between any two points

15:37.000 --> 15:40.000
can be explained as a delta rotation 1,

15:40.000 --> 15:43.000
so you rotate first, then you translate

15:43.000 --> 15:45.000
by delta translation, and then you

15:45.000 --> 15:48.000
do a rotation by delta rotation 2.

15:48.000 --> 15:50.000
So this is just an abstraction over the actual movement.

15:50.000 --> 15:53.000
So for example, if you had a differential drive robot,

15:53.000 --> 15:56.000
maybe the robot actually moves along an arc,

15:56.000 --> 15:58.000
but for the sake of analysis,

15:58.000 --> 16:01.000
what we say is that our control commands

16:01.000 --> 16:03.000
in this case are broken down into this model,

16:03.000 --> 16:06.000
where each motion is basically a rotation,

16:06.000 --> 16:09.000
a translation, and a rotation.

16:09.000 --> 16:12.000
And some equations here to figure out

16:12.000 --> 16:14.000
what these rotation values are.

16:14.000 --> 16:17.000
So we know that we start at this position,

16:17.000 --> 16:19.000
and we end up at this position,

16:19.000 --> 16:22.000
so it's the position and the orientation, so the poles.

16:22.000 --> 16:25.000
And so for example, to figure out what's the translation

16:25.000 --> 16:28.000
that we moved, it's essentially the distance

16:28.000 --> 16:32.000
between these two coordinates, and this is what we see here.

16:32.000 --> 16:35.000
Whereas the angle, so the delta rotation 1,

16:35.000 --> 16:39.000
again can be obtained by taking the tan inverse

16:39.000 --> 16:43.000
of the difference in the y's and the x coordinates,

16:43.000 --> 16:47.000
subtracted by the original orientation that you have.

16:47.000 --> 16:49.000
And similarly, the delta rotation 2

16:49.000 --> 16:53.000
is what's left after removing the delta rotation 1

16:53.000 --> 16:57.000
and the original yaw angle that you had.

16:57.000 --> 17:01.000
So these are just equations for our odometry information.

17:01.000 --> 17:03.000
So from now on in the odometry model,

17:03.000 --> 17:06.000
we'll assume that the odometry commands

17:06.000 --> 17:10.000
are basically defined by these three parameters,

17:10.000 --> 17:13.000
so two rotations and a translation.

17:13.000 --> 17:16.000
So now that we have, now we understand what the poles are,

17:16.000 --> 17:19.000
so the poles in this case are 2D,

17:19.000 --> 17:22.000
the robot lives in a 2D world, so it has an xy position

17:22.000 --> 17:25.000
and a yaw, and the control commands

17:25.000 --> 17:27.000
are these three things here.

17:27.000 --> 17:30.000
So the next thing to understand the uncertainty, so to say,

17:30.000 --> 17:36.000
is to model how the noise is there

17:36.000 --> 17:38.000
on these different parameters.

17:38.000 --> 17:42.000
So one way would be, for example, if you start here,

17:42.000 --> 17:46.000
you could say that we roughly end up here,

17:46.000 --> 17:49.000
but with, let's say, a Gaussian distribution

17:49.000 --> 17:50.000
around this place.

17:50.000 --> 17:51.000
This could be one assumption.

17:51.000 --> 17:54.000
So we start from here, very likely we end up here,

17:54.000 --> 17:57.000
but we might also end up a bit here, a bit here,

17:57.000 --> 17:59.000
and this noise could be Gaussian.

17:59.000 --> 18:02.000
But often in practice, what's seen to work better

18:02.000 --> 18:05.000
is rather than assuming one big motion

18:05.000 --> 18:09.000
which has Gaussian noise, we model the Gaussian noise

18:09.000 --> 18:11.000
on each of these different commands.

18:11.000 --> 18:14.000
So we have a delta rotation 1, a delta translation,

18:14.000 --> 18:16.000
and a delta rotation 2.

18:16.000 --> 18:19.000
And how we model is that there's a Gaussian noise,

18:19.000 --> 18:21.000
and we'll see that, in principle, this

18:21.000 --> 18:22.000
need not be just Gaussian.

18:22.000 --> 18:26.000
So it could be any model which explains your noise well

18:26.000 --> 18:28.000
in reality.

18:28.000 --> 18:31.000
And basically, we would have three separate Gaussian noises,

18:31.000 --> 18:34.000
let's say, on the delta rotation 1, the delta translation 2,

18:34.000 --> 18:36.000
and the delta rotation 2.

18:37.000 --> 18:43.000
And we should note here that the resulting noise that

18:43.000 --> 18:46.000
would end up in the final position

18:46.000 --> 18:49.000
would not be a Gaussian as before,

18:49.000 --> 18:53.000
because the equations like we saw before,

18:53.000 --> 18:56.000
these equations, because these determine how the motion

18:56.000 --> 18:58.000
actually happens, are non-linear.

18:58.000 --> 19:00.000
And therefore, when this Gaussian noise

19:00.000 --> 19:03.000
propagates through these non-linear functions,

19:03.000 --> 19:05.000
we would end up with some function here

19:05.000 --> 19:07.000
which need not be Gaussian.

19:07.000 --> 19:09.000
And the reason we do this is, in practice,

19:09.000 --> 19:12.000
we observe that this particular model

19:12.000 --> 19:17.000
explains the actual noise often better in real world.

19:17.000 --> 19:19.000
So let's go a little bit more into details

19:19.000 --> 19:22.000
of how actually we model this Gaussian noise.

19:22.000 --> 19:25.000
So like we said, so for each of the parameters,

19:25.000 --> 19:27.000
so the delta rotation 1, the delta translation 2,

19:27.000 --> 19:30.000
and the delta rotation 2, we model noise terms.

19:30.000 --> 19:34.000
So this epsilon here models the noise on those parameters.

19:34.000 --> 19:35.000
So these are the mean movements.

19:35.000 --> 19:38.000
So let's say, for example, this is the rotation

19:38.000 --> 19:40.000
that we ask the robot to do.

19:40.000 --> 19:43.000
And then this sets a particular noise on top of it.

19:43.000 --> 19:47.000
And this noise, we model it depending on two terms.

19:47.000 --> 19:51.000
One, this noise depends on how much you ask it to rotate.

19:51.000 --> 19:56.000
So this alpha 1 is kind of a coefficient which says,

19:56.000 --> 19:59.000
so if you ask the robot to rotate 30 degrees,

19:59.000 --> 20:00.000
what's the error?

20:00.000 --> 20:03.000
While if you ask it to rotate 60 degrees, what's the error?

20:03.000 --> 20:07.000
So intuitively, we know that the larger the motion we ask it,

20:07.000 --> 20:10.000
usually the higher the error is.

20:10.000 --> 20:13.000
And in the general or in the typical model,

20:13.000 --> 20:15.000
there's also a certain amount of,

20:15.000 --> 20:18.000
or there's a certain effect of the translation that you make.

20:18.000 --> 20:20.000
So if you make a larger translation,

20:20.000 --> 20:23.000
it's possible that you end up with a rotation error

20:23.000 --> 20:25.000
which is also larger.

20:25.000 --> 20:27.000
Similarly, in the delta translation,

20:27.000 --> 20:34.000
we model the noise in terms of the length that we cover.

20:34.000 --> 20:39.000
And it's also a function of the two rotations that we have.

20:39.000 --> 20:40.000
So we have terms.

20:40.000 --> 20:43.000
So here, basically, we have four terms.

20:43.000 --> 20:48.000
So alpha 1, which, let's say, maps the error in the rotation

20:48.000 --> 20:51.000
part, alpha 2, which maps the error in the translation

20:51.000 --> 20:52.000
parts for this case.

20:52.000 --> 20:55.000
And then we have an alpha 3 and alpha 4 which,

20:55.000 --> 20:58.000
so to say, capture the effects of translation on translation

20:58.000 --> 21:02.000
and rotation on translation and so on.

21:02.000 --> 21:03.000
And again, here, alpha 1 and alpha 2

21:03.000 --> 21:07.000
are the same as in the first case.

21:07.000 --> 21:09.000
So this random variable, in general,

21:09.000 --> 21:12.000
we would assume it in most cases to be normal distributed

21:12.000 --> 21:14.000
because it's the distribution we use most often

21:14.000 --> 21:18.000
and also what is observed in reality.

21:18.000 --> 21:21.000
But nothing stops us from defining this

21:21.000 --> 21:25.000
as any other distribution that might be meaningful.

21:25.000 --> 21:29.000
And for example, one such distribution

21:29.000 --> 21:32.000
is the triangular distribution.

21:32.000 --> 21:35.000
So it looks very much like a normal distribution

21:35.000 --> 21:38.000
apart from the fact that in the normal distribution,

21:38.000 --> 21:41.000
even if you go very far away, even if you're at infinite,

21:41.000 --> 21:45.000
for example, there is a very small probability but still

21:45.000 --> 21:48.000
a probability of you being, let's say,

21:48.000 --> 21:53.000
infinitely away, which in the case of robot's movement

21:53.000 --> 21:57.000
is equivalent to the robot being kidnapped, for example.

21:57.000 --> 21:59.000
If you know that this is something

21:59.000 --> 22:01.000
that won't happen in your system,

22:01.000 --> 22:04.000
you can model your noise using a triangular distribution

22:04.000 --> 22:08.000
where you ensure that your uncertainty is within a bound

22:08.000 --> 22:11.000
and you never go out of these bounds.

22:11.000 --> 22:16.000
And what you see here are basically the equations for how,

22:16.000 --> 22:19.000
basically this is the equation for the normal density function

22:19.000 --> 22:21.000
and this is similarly an equation

22:21.000 --> 22:24.000
for the triangular distribution.

22:24.000 --> 22:26.000
So given all this information,

22:26.000 --> 22:28.000
we want to come back to our main question,

22:28.000 --> 22:31.000
which is to figure out a way to compute

22:31.000 --> 22:33.000
this probability distribution.

22:33.000 --> 22:36.000
So again, we sit at xt-1,

22:36.000 --> 22:40.000
we make a control command,

22:40.000 --> 22:43.000
an odometry command in this case, ut.

22:43.000 --> 22:45.000
So if you consider the odometry model,

22:45.000 --> 22:48.000
this would be a rotation translation and a rotation.

22:48.000 --> 22:52.000
And we want to compute the probability

22:52.000 --> 22:54.000
of ending up at different positions.

22:54.000 --> 22:58.000
So this is the probability that we want to compute overall.

22:58.000 --> 23:01.000
And just a point to note,

23:01.000 --> 23:04.000
how do we actually calculate this probability

23:04.000 --> 23:05.000
at a particular point?

23:05.000 --> 23:08.000
So it's just, again, the equations of a normal distribution

23:08.000 --> 23:10.000
and a triangular distribution.

23:10.000 --> 23:14.000
But what we see here in terms of a and b

23:14.000 --> 23:17.000
are what are here called as query points

23:17.000 --> 23:19.000
and standard deviation.

23:19.000 --> 23:21.000
So the query point in our case would be,

23:21.000 --> 23:26.000
suppose we expect the robot to end up at, say, x prime.

23:26.000 --> 23:30.000
The query point would be, say, for any given x.

23:30.000 --> 23:33.000
So x minus x prime would be, so to say,

23:33.000 --> 23:36.000
how far you are away from your mean expectation.

23:36.000 --> 23:38.000
And for that particular query point,

23:38.000 --> 23:41.000
this would be the probability distribution

23:41.000 --> 23:43.000
that's given by the normal equation.

23:43.000 --> 23:45.000
Similarly, if you use a triangular distribution,

23:45.000 --> 23:49.000
this would be that particular probability distribution.

23:49.000 --> 23:54.000
So, for example, now we ask the robot to move 30 degrees.

23:54.000 --> 23:57.000
So the mean movement that we expect is 30 degrees.

23:57.000 --> 23:59.000
And suppose I ask,

23:59.000 --> 24:02.000
what's the probability that it actually moved 45 degrees?

24:02.000 --> 24:07.000
Then the a would be 45 degrees minus 30 degrees.

24:07.000 --> 24:10.000
Whereas the b is, so to say, the standard deviation

24:10.000 --> 24:12.000
which we need to understand

24:12.000 --> 24:15.000
from the motion of the robot itself.

24:15.000 --> 24:18.000
So usually what's the error that we expect

24:18.000 --> 24:21.000
in this particular movement that's there?

24:21.000 --> 24:25.000
So now that we have this, we come out with an algorithm

24:25.000 --> 24:28.000
which actually summarizes the procedure

24:28.000 --> 24:31.000
of computing these probabilities.

24:31.000 --> 24:35.000
And so the result of this algorithm

24:35.000 --> 24:39.000
is this probability distribution that we want to compute.

24:39.000 --> 24:41.000
And in this case, we want to compute it

24:41.000 --> 24:44.000
between a hypothesis x and x prime.

24:44.000 --> 24:47.000
So our hypothesis is that we start at time t minus one

24:47.000 --> 24:51.000
at x and we end up at x prime.

24:51.000 --> 24:54.000
And the x bar and the x bar prime

24:54.000 --> 24:58.000
are what we know from our odometry measurements.

24:58.000 --> 25:01.000
So first what we do is basically

25:01.000 --> 25:03.000
convert our odometry measurements

25:03.000 --> 25:06.000
into our odometry model commands, so to say.

25:06.000 --> 25:09.000
So what we have here is that if we had actually moved

25:09.000 --> 25:14.000
from x bar to x bar prime,

25:14.000 --> 25:17.000
this would be the equations that we exactly saw.

25:17.000 --> 25:21.000
So these are the movements in terms of the translation

25:21.000 --> 25:24.000
and the two rotations.

25:24.000 --> 25:29.000
We compute exactly the same things for our hypothesis.

25:29.000 --> 25:31.000
So if we want to figure out what's the probability

25:31.000 --> 25:35.000
that we end up at x prime given that we started at x,

25:35.000 --> 25:38.000
we again convert this into our model,

25:38.000 --> 25:41.000
which are the rotation, translation, and rotation.

25:41.000 --> 25:45.000
And then what we can then do is for each of these movements,

25:45.000 --> 25:47.000
so the rotation, translation, and rotation,

25:47.000 --> 25:51.000
we can compute the probability of these movements

25:51.000 --> 25:52.000
actually having happened.

25:52.000 --> 25:55.000
And we can compute it given the mean position

25:55.000 --> 25:57.000
or the difference to the mean position here

25:57.000 --> 26:01.000
and the noise that we modeled on that particular motion.

26:01.000 --> 26:04.000
And then we assume here that these three motions

26:04.000 --> 26:06.000
are independent of each other.

26:06.000 --> 26:09.000
And therefore, for this particular hypothesis,

26:09.000 --> 26:12.000
the probability distribution ends up being

26:12.000 --> 26:15.000
the multiplication of these three probabilities

26:15.000 --> 26:16.000
that you saw here.

26:16.000 --> 26:19.000
So what we did here is to have kind of an algorithm

26:19.000 --> 26:21.000
to figure out what's the probability

26:21.000 --> 26:24.000
of moving to x prime from x.

26:24.000 --> 26:27.000
And then we would need to repeat this for all the positions

26:27.000 --> 26:31.000
in the environment for which we want to compute

26:31.000 --> 26:34.000
this motion model probability.

26:34.000 --> 26:37.000
And if you visualize the result, what we end up

26:37.000 --> 26:39.000
is something like here.

26:39.000 --> 26:41.000
So we start here at x.

26:41.000 --> 26:44.000
Then we give a command U.

26:44.000 --> 26:46.000
And what you see here, it's a visualization

26:46.000 --> 26:48.000
of the probability distribution.

26:48.000 --> 26:53.000
So we see that this larger or the more black areas

26:53.000 --> 26:55.000
here correspond to the place where

26:55.000 --> 26:58.000
you are more likely to end up.

26:58.000 --> 27:02.000
And the places away from the center

27:02.000 --> 27:04.000
are lighter, representing the fact

27:04.000 --> 27:06.000
that you are less likely to end up there.

27:06.000 --> 27:09.000
And the fact that this is shaped in this what is often

27:09.000 --> 27:13.000
called as a banana-shaped distribution is in this case

27:13.000 --> 27:18.000
because the robot might have a greater error in the rotation

27:18.000 --> 27:21.000
components than the translation one.

27:21.000 --> 27:26.000
That's why we see there's more variance along the angles

27:26.000 --> 27:29.000
with respect to along the translation vector.

27:29.000 --> 27:34.000
So in case if all the errors were, say, exactly the same,

27:34.000 --> 27:37.000
we would see a more circular distribution here.

27:37.000 --> 27:39.000
And of course, this depends on the path

27:39.000 --> 27:42.000
that the robot itself takes because it's something

27:42.000 --> 27:44.000
that's propagating over time.

27:44.000 --> 27:47.000
So for example, if we take this particular path here,

27:47.000 --> 27:49.000
we might end up with the shape which

27:49.000 --> 27:52.000
looks like something like here.

27:52.000 --> 27:53.000
So in this case, what we actually

27:53.000 --> 27:57.000
did is to go to each one of these places.

27:57.000 --> 28:00.000
So for example, we make a grid around, say,

28:00.000 --> 28:02.000
around the final position.

28:02.000 --> 28:04.000
And then for each one of these grids,

28:04.000 --> 28:08.000
we basically computed this algorithm.

28:08.000 --> 28:10.000
And then we ended up with different values.

28:10.000 --> 28:13.000
So here, of course, it's plotted in 2D.

28:13.000 --> 28:18.000
But in practice, it's a 3D case where you have the x, the y,

28:18.000 --> 28:20.000
and the orientation.

28:20.000 --> 28:22.000
So this is one kind of representation,

28:22.000 --> 28:25.000
also sometimes called as the histogram representation

28:25.000 --> 28:28.000
because we basically discretize our world

28:28.000 --> 28:31.000
and we have one probability for each one

28:31.000 --> 28:32.000
of this grid in the world.

28:32.000 --> 28:35.000
Another way to represent this is through what

28:35.000 --> 28:38.000
is called as a sample-based representation.

28:38.000 --> 28:41.000
Essentially, what we see is a general probability

28:41.000 --> 28:43.000
distribution function.

28:43.000 --> 28:47.000
And the sample representation would be the blue things

28:47.000 --> 28:48.000
that you see here.

28:48.000 --> 28:51.000
And the idea here is that the places where there is a higher

28:51.000 --> 28:56.000
density, we have more number of samples being sampled

28:56.000 --> 28:57.000
from there.

28:57.000 --> 29:00.000
And in some of the Bayesian implementations

29:00.000 --> 29:02.000
that we'll see in this course, we'll

29:02.000 --> 29:05.000
see that it's better to have a sample-based representation

29:05.000 --> 29:07.000
than a histogram-like representation

29:07.000 --> 29:09.000
like we have before.

29:09.000 --> 29:13.000
So what we want to compute in terms of, let's say,

29:13.000 --> 29:17.000
the motion model is we want a sample-based representation

29:17.000 --> 29:19.000
for the motion model.

29:19.000 --> 29:23.000
And this requires us to first know

29:23.000 --> 29:25.000
how to sample different distributions.

29:25.000 --> 29:29.000
And the first distribution that we would

29:29.000 --> 29:34.000
like to sample from is a normal distribution.

29:34.000 --> 29:36.000
And there happens to be a very simple way

29:36.000 --> 29:37.000
to do this distribution.

29:37.000 --> 29:41.000
So if you have a standard deviation of b,

29:41.000 --> 29:44.000
the way to compute these samples would just

29:44.000 --> 29:48.000
take 12 of such uniformly distributed numbers,

29:48.000 --> 29:51.000
add them up, and divide by 2.

29:51.000 --> 29:53.000
So this gives a distribution which

29:53.000 --> 29:55.000
looks something like this.

29:55.000 --> 29:57.000
So here we take a million samples.

29:57.000 --> 30:01.000
And as we can see, the distribution of these samples

30:01.000 --> 30:04.000
looks like a normal distribution.

30:04.000 --> 30:07.000
And we can do this for a triangular distribution as well.

30:07.000 --> 30:08.000
So it happens.

30:08.000 --> 30:10.000
We won't look into the proof, but basically it

30:11.000 --> 30:14.000
happens that if we compute a sample

30:14.000 --> 30:17.000
from this particular formula here,

30:17.000 --> 30:19.000
we can end up with a triangular distribution

30:19.000 --> 30:20.000
which looks like this.

30:20.000 --> 30:23.000
For example, if we have only 1,000 samples,

30:23.000 --> 30:26.000
there's still some error in terms of approximation.

30:26.000 --> 30:28.000
But the more samples we take, the more closer

30:28.000 --> 30:31.000
we get to an actual distribution.

30:31.000 --> 30:32.000
So here we have a million samples,

30:32.000 --> 30:36.000
and we are quite close to a distribution that looks

30:36.000 --> 30:38.000
like a triangular distribution.

30:38.000 --> 30:41.000
And when we have this sample-based model,

30:41.000 --> 30:43.000
we could think of them as a simulation.

30:43.000 --> 30:47.000
So suppose we have 100 samples where we start from.

30:47.000 --> 30:50.000
So these would be 100 different Xt minus 1s.

30:50.000 --> 30:54.000
And then for each of the sample, we

30:54.000 --> 30:56.000
compute the motion for each of the sample

30:56.000 --> 31:00.000
and add the noise based on how we model the noise.

31:00.000 --> 31:03.000
And we end up with these new poses.

31:03.000 --> 31:05.000
So for each one of the poses, or each one

31:05.000 --> 31:07.000
of the robot position Xt minus 1,

31:07.000 --> 31:11.000
we make the motion, we sample some noise around that motion,

31:11.000 --> 31:15.000
and this would give us a new probability distribution

31:15.000 --> 31:18.000
of where you will end up with, so what the motion model

31:18.000 --> 31:19.000
actually describes.

31:19.000 --> 31:22.000
And we can summarize this in an algorithm here.

31:22.000 --> 31:24.000
So what you have is your position

31:24.000 --> 31:29.000
at time t minus 1, which is X, and then the control command U.

31:29.000 --> 31:31.000
Again, in the odometry model, this

31:31.000 --> 31:34.000
would be a rotation translation and a rotation.

31:34.000 --> 31:39.000
And what we do is then the first motion can be thought of

31:39.000 --> 31:42.000
as a rotation that we actually want.

31:42.000 --> 31:46.000
And then we can sample from one of the noise distributions

31:46.000 --> 31:48.000
that we saw before.

31:48.000 --> 31:52.000
So these noise distributions, for example, in this case

31:52.000 --> 31:56.000
is exactly the one that we defined before, the one which

31:56.000 --> 31:58.000
depends on the amount of rotation that you give

31:58.000 --> 32:00.000
and also on the translations that you give.

32:00.000 --> 32:03.000
And we again repeat this for all the three motions.

32:03.000 --> 32:06.000
And then what we do is basically

32:06.000 --> 32:11.000
we propagate these motions using the motion model.

32:11.000 --> 32:14.000
So what we have here is that if we start at X,

32:14.000 --> 32:17.000
we end up at X prime, which is nothing but X

32:17.000 --> 32:21.000
plus the translations that we took times the cosine

32:21.000 --> 32:26.000
of the angle plus the rotation that it made,

32:26.000 --> 32:28.000
including the noise that we sampled.

32:28.000 --> 32:30.000
So we will end up in place that we expect

32:30.000 --> 32:32.000
plus some noise around it.

32:32.000 --> 32:37.000
And so what this will help us is to come up

32:37.000 --> 32:40.000
with a new X prime, Y prime, and theta prime,

32:40.000 --> 32:44.000
which would be the place where the particle would end up with.

32:44.000 --> 32:46.000
And in this case here, this sampling

32:46.000 --> 32:48.000
could be from different distributions.

32:48.000 --> 32:51.000
It could be from a normal distribution, as it says here,

32:51.000 --> 32:54.000
or a triangular distribution, or some other fancy distribution

32:54.000 --> 32:58.000
if you come up with which describes your motion well.

32:58.000 --> 33:01.000
So how this looks in the end is what

33:01.000 --> 33:02.000
we see in the bottom here.

33:02.000 --> 33:05.000
So before we saw this histogram kind of distribution,

33:05.000 --> 33:08.000
where we had, let's say, the shade of black here,

33:08.000 --> 33:10.000
the shade of gray here, representing how probable

33:10.000 --> 33:13.000
it is to reach here, what we see here

33:13.000 --> 33:16.000
is these are the different samples, which

33:16.000 --> 33:19.000
could be the places where you would end up with.

33:19.000 --> 33:21.000
So you can see roughly that the shape

33:21.000 --> 33:23.000
is similar to the histogram one.

33:23.000 --> 33:26.000
And in each of these cases, basically it

33:26.000 --> 33:29.000
characterizes different noise parameters.

33:29.000 --> 33:32.000
So in this case, there's more error on the angle,

33:32.000 --> 33:34.000
so it's more banana shaped.

33:34.000 --> 33:37.000
Whereas in this case, it seems that the robot

33:37.000 --> 33:40.000
has more error along the direction in which it moves.

33:40.000 --> 33:42.000
And therefore, the error is larger,

33:42.000 --> 33:45.000
the variance is larger along the motion.

33:45.000 --> 33:47.000
Whereas again, in this case, it seems

33:47.000 --> 33:51.000
that the error again in the angles

33:51.000 --> 33:55.000
is much larger than the one in translations.

33:55.000 --> 33:58.000
So let's see a simple example where

33:58.000 --> 34:01.000
what happens here is that the robot starts at this position

34:01.000 --> 34:05.000
and it executes this particular trajectory here.

34:05.000 --> 34:07.000
And when you start, typically it happens

34:07.000 --> 34:09.000
that you know exactly where you start from,

34:09.000 --> 34:12.000
and then you execute a motion.

34:12.000 --> 34:15.000
In this case, we show or we represent this

34:15.000 --> 34:18.000
using the sampling based approach.

34:18.000 --> 34:20.000
And we execute the first motion, we

34:20.000 --> 34:23.000
see that we have more uncertainty, which

34:23.000 --> 34:27.000
is captured by the spread of these particles or these samples.

34:27.000 --> 34:29.000
And then we execute another motion and so on.

34:29.000 --> 34:33.000
And after executing this for whatever 10 commands,

34:33.000 --> 34:35.000
we end up with a probability distribution

34:35.000 --> 34:37.000
which is quite spread.

34:37.000 --> 34:39.000
So we could have done or we have done here

34:39.000 --> 34:41.000
in this case using the samples idea,

34:41.000 --> 34:45.000
but we could have also done the same using the histogram idea

34:45.000 --> 34:48.000
where we would query for each point in the environment

34:48.000 --> 34:51.000
or in the configuration space what's

34:51.000 --> 34:54.000
the probability of ending up there.

34:54.000 --> 34:56.000
So we've in general seen two approaches.

34:56.000 --> 35:01.000
One where we look for each, so to say, grid in our environment

35:01.000 --> 35:04.000
and compute this probability distribution.

35:04.000 --> 35:08.000
So p of xt given xt minus one and ut.

35:08.000 --> 35:10.000
We've also seen a simulation kind

35:10.000 --> 35:16.000
of where we add this noise while doing the movement.

35:16.000 --> 35:19.000
So we'll also quickly look at the other model

35:19.000 --> 35:23.000
that we mentioned before, so the velocity based model.

35:23.000 --> 35:26.000
Here basically what happens is that the command

35:26.000 --> 35:29.000
is described by the linear and the angular velocity

35:29.000 --> 35:30.000
for that robot.

35:30.000 --> 35:33.000
So when you set a linear and an angular velocity,

35:33.000 --> 35:37.000
this robot moves along some circular arc.

35:37.000 --> 35:42.000
So this is also one way to model how a robot can move.

35:42.000 --> 35:46.000
And in this case, the way we model the noise

35:46.000 --> 35:49.000
is on these parameters v and omega.

35:49.000 --> 35:51.000
So v being the linear velocity and omega

35:51.000 --> 35:52.000
being the angular velocity.

35:52.000 --> 35:55.000
So we could say that our linear velocity

35:55.000 --> 35:57.000
is affected by some amount of noise,

35:57.000 --> 35:59.000
here shown by this epsilon.

35:59.000 --> 36:02.000
Again, this epsilon depends on, let's say,

36:02.000 --> 36:04.000
the magnitude of the velocity of the linear

36:04.000 --> 36:05.000
and the angular velocity.

36:05.000 --> 36:08.000
And similarly, the error on the angular velocity

36:08.000 --> 36:12.000
again also depends on both these magnitudes, each one of them

36:12.000 --> 36:14.000
having their own coefficients.

36:14.000 --> 36:17.000
However, there's a small problem with this method

36:17.000 --> 36:21.000
in the sense that the state that we want to track

36:21.000 --> 36:23.000
is having three dimensions.

36:23.000 --> 36:26.000
So we want to track the x, the y, and the orientation.

36:26.000 --> 36:29.000
The controls here are only two.

36:29.000 --> 36:32.000
So there's a missing dimension, so to say.

36:32.000 --> 36:34.000
And the way to think about this is that

36:34.000 --> 36:39.000
if we give a particular linear and an angular velocity,

36:39.000 --> 36:44.000
this corresponds to a particular arc that this robot traverses.

36:44.000 --> 36:47.000
But what we are missing is the orientation

36:47.000 --> 36:49.000
along that arc that it travels.

36:49.000 --> 36:53.000
So you could end up, say, at the same position on this arc

36:53.000 --> 36:55.000
but having different orientations.

36:55.000 --> 36:58.000
And with this model, we lack the ability

36:58.000 --> 37:03.000
to model this error on this third orientation parameter.

37:03.000 --> 37:05.000
So what's often done in practice is

37:05.000 --> 37:10.000
to have an extra error term here shown by gamma,

37:10.000 --> 37:15.000
which we'll then see is added to the final orientation

37:15.000 --> 37:17.000
of the system.

37:17.000 --> 37:20.000
So here, we won't go through the proof

37:20.000 --> 37:21.000
of how these equations come up.

37:21.000 --> 37:24.000
But basically, these are the motion update equations

37:24.000 --> 37:26.000
when we use the velocity model.

37:26.000 --> 37:29.000
So the commands are basically v and omega.

37:29.000 --> 37:33.000
Here, they're actually v hat and omega are the ones

37:33.000 --> 37:36.000
which have the noise already added to them.

37:36.000 --> 37:38.000
And so here, what happens is that if you start at x,

37:38.000 --> 37:41.000
and then you apply a particular command, v and omega,

37:41.000 --> 37:44.000
you end up at this x prime.

37:44.000 --> 37:47.000
And this guy also, or these equations,

37:47.000 --> 37:52.000
also include the noise as we model the noise in the v

37:52.000 --> 37:55.000
using this v hat here, which captures

37:55.000 --> 37:57.000
the noise in the linear velocity,

37:57.000 --> 37:59.000
where this parameter captures the noise

37:59.000 --> 38:02.000
in the angular velocity, and this gamma term,

38:02.000 --> 38:07.000
which would capture then the error in the orientation.

38:07.000 --> 38:12.000
So with this, we have a model for describing

38:12.000 --> 38:16.000
the noise for a velocity-based model for wheeled robots,

38:16.000 --> 38:17.000
mainly.

38:17.000 --> 38:20.000
And what the results would look like is what we see here.

38:20.000 --> 38:23.000
So visually, they look very similar to what

38:23.000 --> 38:27.000
we saw from our odometry model.

38:27.000 --> 38:30.000
But for certain robots, it's not possible

38:30.000 --> 38:34.000
to say the robots are not equipped with some encoders.

38:34.000 --> 38:38.000
In that case, we often use this velocity model,

38:38.000 --> 38:41.000
since all we have is the commands that we give them.

38:41.000 --> 38:44.000
And in practice, like I said before,

38:44.000 --> 38:47.000
usually these velocity models are less accurate

38:47.000 --> 38:51.000
than the odometry model, since this is, so to say,

38:51.000 --> 38:55.000
apply command, and then just you don't know what happens,

38:55.000 --> 38:59.000
rather than having a feedback about how much the robots move.

38:59.000 --> 39:02.000
So we saw basically two different kinds of models,

39:02.000 --> 39:04.000
an odometry model where we have these revolutions,

39:04.000 --> 39:08.000
and therefore we can figure out how much the wheels move,

39:08.000 --> 39:11.000
and this model where we just know the commands

39:11.000 --> 39:12.000
and how the robot moves.

39:12.000 --> 39:17.000
So with this, what we have is ways to describe the motion,

39:17.000 --> 39:22.000
as well as to describe the uncertainty about that motion.

39:22.000 --> 39:26.000
And then when multiple of these commands are executed,

39:26.000 --> 39:29.000
these errors add up, resulting in the drift,

39:29.000 --> 39:33.000
like we saw in the initial image.

39:33.000 --> 39:36.000
So just to summarize, what we saw are two kinds of models,

39:36.000 --> 39:38.000
so the odometry-based models,

39:38.000 --> 39:40.000
where we have encoders on the motors,

39:40.000 --> 39:43.000
a velocity-based model where we just know the commands

39:43.000 --> 39:46.000
that we gave to the robot.

39:46.000 --> 39:48.000
And then we discussed a couple of ways

39:48.000 --> 39:51.000
to calculate this probabilistic motion model,

39:51.000 --> 39:56.000
so what we can also call as the posterior probability

39:56.000 --> 39:59.000
of ending up at x prime, given that you start at x

39:59.000 --> 40:03.000
and you apply a command of u.

40:03.000 --> 40:06.000
We saw a model where we can query for each point

40:06.000 --> 40:09.000
in the space to figure out what's the probability

40:09.000 --> 40:12.000
of ending up there, but we also saw another method

40:12.000 --> 40:15.000
where we can propagate a sample to figure out

40:15.000 --> 40:18.000
where we would end up in a simulated style affair.

40:18.000 --> 40:21.000
And both of these methods we'll see in further lectures

40:21.000 --> 40:24.000
how to use them for state estimation problems.

40:24.000 --> 40:28.000
And typically, just a note here is that all the calculations

40:28.000 --> 40:32.000
that we do is given a fixed time interval, delta t,

40:32.000 --> 40:35.000
so we assume that these odometry commands hold true

40:35.000 --> 40:37.000
for that fixed time interval, delta t,

40:37.000 --> 40:39.000
and in practice, if we do this fast enough,

40:39.000 --> 40:43.000
say 100 times a second, it's almost equivalent

40:43.000 --> 40:48.000
to having a continuous integration of these commands.

40:48.000 --> 40:50.000
So with this, I would also like to point you

40:50.000 --> 40:52.000
to the literature for this lecture,

40:52.000 --> 40:54.000
so if you would like to know more details

40:54.000 --> 40:58.000
or figure out the proof for, let's say, the velocity model,

40:58.000 --> 41:02.000
you could refer to the Chapter 5 in Probabilistic Robotics book,

41:02.000 --> 41:04.000
and in general, it's a really nice book to understand

41:04.000 --> 41:07.000
the different concepts that we cover in this lecture.

41:07.000 --> 41:09.000
With this, I thank you for your attention

41:09.000 --> 41:11.000
and see you in the next lecture.

